**Structural property (the reason this gate exists where it does).** G-S1-GO is a **cut
vertex** over the S1 implementation subgraph: `F-EXTRACT-FIX`, `I-STORE` and `I-OUTBOX` all
carry it in `deps`, and `I-EXTRACT`/`I-INGEST`/`T-ROUNDTRIP`/`M-S1` are transitively behind
those. No implementation node in S1 is reachable without traversing C4. It sits at the head
of the declared `critical_path` (`[S-PROBE, G-S1-GO, F-EXTRACT-FIX, …]`), so its latency is
founder wall-clock, not compute (§9 Performance).

**Deliberately *not* cut:** `S-PROBE` (w0), `F-ENVELOPE` (w0) and `F-BUNDLE` (w1) run
pre-gate. That is correct — they are contract-freezing, and freezing a schema is what makes
the probe's findings testable. It also creates real sunk-cost exposure on `REVERT`, which the
design surfaces rather than hides (§7 gap G3, §9 Risk Officer).

